<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  OPTIONAL root helper: restores Background Task Management coverage on macOS 26.

  macOS 26 moved `sfltool dumpbtm` behind system.privilege.admin, so the
  unprivileged aegis agent can no longer read the SMAppService store (the
  modern persistence path: a login item registered via the API that never drops
  a plist in ~/Library/LaunchAgents). aegis reports that honestly as a
  PRIVILEGED coverage gap rather than pretending the surface is empty.

  The gap cannot be closed by clicking Allow: the authorization right is
  `shared: false` (no credential transfers to the fresh sfltool aegis spawns)
  with `timeout: 300` (a cached grant dies before the next 600s scan). But it
  is `allow-root: true` -- a process already running as root is authorized with
  no prompt at all. So ONLY the dump runs as root, on a schedule, into a
  root-owned file; aegis itself stays unprivileged and merely reads it.

  Do NOT instead make system.privilege.admin passwordless: that right gates
  AuthorizationExecuteWithPrivileges, so weakening it to silence one monitor
  hands every installer on the machine silent root.

  The dump is written tmp+mv so a scan can never read a half-written file, and
  aegis refuses any dump not owned by root -- otherwise this file would become
  a way for an attacker to forge the background-item list and hide persistence.

  Install: see the `btm-daemon` section of README.md.
-->
<plist version="1.0"><dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>com.charlie.aegis-btm</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/sh</string>
    <string>-c</string>
    <string>umask 022; /usr/bin/sfltool dumpbtm &gt; /var/db/aegis/btm.txt.tmp 2&gt;/dev/null &amp;&amp; /bin/mv -f /var/db/aegis/btm.txt.tmp /var/db/aegis/btm.txt</string>
  </array>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><true/>
  <key>StartInterval</key><integer>1800</integer>
</dict></plist>
